WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.900
- Hi everyone. Today is the
day we would have gathered

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.360
on Old Campus to celebrate
our achievements as a class.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.160
We wish we could all be together,

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.400
but we hope you've still
been able to enjoy,

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.923
reminisce and celebrate our
class over this past week.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.820
We have found many new
ways of celebrating,

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.270
but we've also kept many traditions.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.240
One of the traditions we are
excited to be carrying on

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.080
is the announcement of our
classes' prize winners.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.370
Although we will be
honoring these recipients

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.010
when we meet again for
our in person ceremony.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.960
Please join us now in congratulating them

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.748
on their incredible achievement.

00:00:44.748 --> 00:00:47.331
(upbeat music)

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.780
Congratulations to all of our winners

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.320
and to the entire Class of 2020.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.690
Tomorrow we will officially
become graduates of Yale College

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.140
when President Salovey
confers our degrees.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.190
- This is a particularly noteworthy year,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.860
as it is the 50th anniversary
of co-education at Yale,

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.150
and the 150th anniversary of co-education

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.790
at the university as a whole.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.430
In this spirit, we are
delighted to announce

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.930
our renowned Class Day
speaker, Dr. Jean Bennett,

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:42.680
one of the first women to
graduate from Yale College.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.960
Dr. Bennett is a true pioneer
in the field of gene therapy,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.160
and has dedicated her career
to restoring eyesight.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.210
As a daughter of a Yale professor,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.800
Dr. Bennett immersed herself
in the life of the university

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.170
long before she became a Yalie herself

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.300
After graduating from Yale in 1976,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.000
with a Bachelor of
Science degree in biology,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.950
Dr. Bennett earned a PhD in zoology,

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.080
and cell and developmental biology

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.900
from the University of
California, Berkeley,

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.090
and her MD from Harvard.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:20.110
She then returned to New Haven
as a postdoctoral researcher

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.970
living in Silliman College
while her father served

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.970
as the head of the college in the 1980s.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.543
She completed her training
at Johns Hopkins University.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.180
- Her most noteworthy accomplishment to-date

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.390
comes from her own work on gene therapy.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.080
Dr. Bennett created a gene therapy

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.880
for leber congenital amaurosis, LCA,

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.620
which is an hereditary disease
that causes early onset

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.140
and progressive vision loss.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.610
In 2017, the treatment became
the first ever gene therapy

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.670
to be approved by the U.S.
Food and Drug Administration,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.790
paving the way for other forms
applications of gene therapy.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.930
And now as F.M. Kirby
Professor of Ophthalmology

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.620
at the University of Pennsylvania,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.790
Dr. Bennett and her team
are working on developing

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.210
a vaccine for COVID-19.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:07.120
She is truly inspiring example
of how Yale alumni continue

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.190
to make a difference in the world.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.350
And so without further ado,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.823
please help me welcome Dr. Jean Bennett.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.930
- I was told that many of
you would be wearing masks.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.180
So I tried on a few of my favorites,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.150
but none of them were right
for this, or COVID-19.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.690
So here we go.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:28.490
Welcome to the first ever in

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.120
Yale College Class Day at
home or wherever you may be.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.127
It is such an incredible
honor to welcome you

00:04:34.127 --> 00:04:37.770
and to be one of the first to
congratulate the Class of 2020

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.460
and your families on this very special day

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.253
in this unusual venue.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.040
I'd like to give special
thanks to President Salovey,

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.430
dean of Yale College, Marvin Chun,

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.340
writer, lecturer and communications
officer Alison Coleman,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.880
and the Class of 2020
students planning this event.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.620
Calvin Schwartzberg, Ananya
Indwa, Nathan Isaacs,

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.480
Sarah Geach and Michelle Hu,
thank you for this wonderful

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.000
opportunity to share the event with you.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.650
And congratulations to the faculty, deans

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:10.920
and heads of colleges for
all the efforts you've made

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.610
on behalf of the students
and their families,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.430
over the past four years,
culminating in the extraordinary

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:18.680
events in the past few months,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.773
and these last celebratory days.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.830
As I was gathering my thoughts
together for this event,

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.730
I told my husband, this is
really difficult to put together,

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:32.290
especially how the world has
changed in the past few weeks.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:35.137
To which my husband, a
Princeton graduate replied,

00:05:35.137 --> 00:05:38.517
"Don't worry, it's just
Yale, not Princeton."

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.460
Five months ago, who could have imagined

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.420
the events that have transpired?

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.760
I remember thinking, wow, this
is the year we'll remember

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.020
because of the terrible fires
that consumed exotic animals

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.890
in Australia, who could have
predicted that the entire world

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:55.250
would be faced with the calamity

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.790
of even greater proportions,
that we'd all be wearing masks

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:00.450
hoarding supplies and being prevented

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.570
from going to work or school.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.230
This 2020 Class Day experience
demonstrates the adaptiveness

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.770
of Yalies, perhaps a virtual Class Day

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.423
will become a new Yale tradition.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.170
Congratulations, new graduates.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:14.560
First, I hope that you're all

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.450
taking good care of yourselves.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.410
My heart goes out to
those of you whose friends

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.360
and families have suffered
the terrible COVID-19 illness

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.210
and to all of you have
suffered the fallout

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.670
from the need for social isolation.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:26.980
I hope that you're all adjusting

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.033
and adapting as well as possible.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.540
If it didn't before the
number 2020 now marks

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.630
and provides extra meaning
to all of our lives.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.300
I enjoy thinking of the
order and beauty and symmetry

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.770
of numbers, the incidental
or coincidental associations

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.420
of those numbers and how
they impact our lives,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:47.980
our work and the things
that give us pleasure.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.580
The number 2020 has held
special meaning for me,

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.000
given my career and experiences
reversing blindness,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.480
the number brings to
mind perfect eyesight,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.280
seeing things clearly.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.210
Good acuity provides us with the platform

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.940
To see fine detail, to study and to work

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:06.890
and communicate clearly, to
affirm what is really important.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.270
Is a Shelter in Place Order
going to prevent you all

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.970
from celebrating your accomplishments

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.970
and moving forward with your dreams?

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.140
Hell no!

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.550
Yale Class of 2020 will
lead the way to clarity

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:18.930
and solutions for the challenges

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.770
that we have all been
facing with your resilience,

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.620
energy, creativity and brilliance.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:27.750
I hope your experience at
Yale has been fantastic

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.180
and that you've had
numerous new adventures

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.860
that you've gained insights
about yourself in the world

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:34.900
and that you've explored new fields

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:36.840
taken on new challenges and made friends

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.480
with many, many people
with different backgrounds,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.180
talents, hobbies and experiences.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.120
One thing for sure is that
the grand finale is unusual,

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.010
if not memorable, your
experience has been marked

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:52.180
by a new divide in
history before corona, BC

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.640
and after corona, AC.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.320
From a Yale history perspective,

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.500
this makes the early college
dismissal in April 1775

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.180
during the American
Revolution seem trivial.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.620
Although none of these compare
with what we're experiencing

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.480
now, I witnessed many
disruptions while growing up

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:12.020
in New Haven and then attending Yale.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.720
During the Vietnam War
era in the late 60s,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.330
there were disruptions on
all campuses in this country

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.910
due to anti war, dissent
and resistance to the draft.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:24.250
In 1970, New Haven was absorbed
with criminal prosecutions

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.830
against various members
of the Black Panther Party

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.660
that had its headquarters
on Orchard Street.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.640
This case provoked national attention

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.490
and 10s of thousands of supporters

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:35.680
of the Panthers arrived in New Haven.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:37.160
Those were challenging times,

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:39.820
but I was moved by the
thoughtfulness, compassion

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.070
and courage of many Yalies in feeding

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:43.593
and housing some of the crowd.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.190
I know it must be really
difficult imagining what Yale

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.580
and New Haven were like 50 years ago,

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.140
but growing up here with a
father who was a faculty member

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.740
and mother who ran one of the first

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.610
International Baccalaureate
programs in the country

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:58.570
was pretty special.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:02.100
My brother, Bill, sister Nancy
and I hung out on weekends

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.690
at the Peabody Museum.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.910
Saw movies at Linsly Chitt
took our first swim lessons

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.290
at the Payne Whitney Gym.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:11.260
We did all this unintended by an adult.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:13.390
My first volunteer job in seventh grade

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.230
was to feed vampire bats
at Kline Biology Tower

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:19.750
with bowls of blood, and that
was followed by other jobs.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.520
I even got to test intro bio lab protocols

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.930
in advance of the incoming freshmen.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:27.290
Occasionally, I’d serve as
a setup for demonstrations

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.560
my father gave on laser physics

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.880
for one lecture in Dunham auditorium.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.730
He asked if anybody wanted to volunteer

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:36.630
to have a wart removed by his argon laser.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.220
I raised my hand.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.540
Another person the audience did too.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:42.810
Fortunately, I was selected.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.390
My father had a prosthetic
arm behind the podium

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.430
that was dressed for the same
sweater that I was wearing.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:51.000
With me behind the podium leaning over,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.140
he proceeded to burn the
prosthetic arm in half

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:55.450
with the laser.

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:58.780
Although one person yelled,
"Get away from him, he's crazy".

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:00.580
No one came to my rescue.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:03.160
But they all gave you a hand at the end.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.830
There were countless other
wonderful experiences.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.830
One of the most memorable was
participating in the premiere

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.840
of Leonard Bernstein's
Mass with John Mauceri

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:14.940
and Leonard Bernstein in
the Vienna Opera House

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:16.963
that clinched my wish to go to Yale.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.180
Imagine what Yale was like
when I was an undergrad?

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:25.450
There were only four elements
fire, water, earth, air.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:26.483
Well, not quite.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.560
But there were only occasional
landline rotary phones,

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.270
no cell phones, no 911, not
even answering machines.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.240
Webs belonged to spiders.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.410
I used a slide rule
that my dad had given me

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:42.510
for my 16th birthday
for my calculus class.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:46.160
There was no email, the
cloud was still in the sky.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:49.510
We left messages on message
boards or notes under the door.

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:51.830
There were no CDs or DVDs.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:55.270
I had a 33 and a third
phonograph and a tape recorder.

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:56.950
And we used to watch the Evening News

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:59.670
with Walter Cronkite in
the common room on a TV

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.150
that had a dozen channels,

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:03.610
only three of which had good reception,

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:05.920
tweets and Twitter's were only sounds.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.540
There were no digital photos.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.060
We use cameras with film that
we develop in the darkroom

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.140
or send away to be developed.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:14.940
5G was part of an apartment address.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.970
Tinder is what we use to light fires.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.650
Grindr is how we sharpen our tools.

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.730
Applications were documents that you sent

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:24.410
when applying for jobs.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:28.710
There were no emoticons or
emojis and certainly no Google,

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:32.220
Facebook, TikTok or Snapchat.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.680
Imagine this on the 50th
anniversary of co-education

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.010
and the 150th anniversary
of women at Yale,

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:40.790
there were no women.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.990
Growing up in New Haven, I had no idea

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.700
that it might even be
possible for me to go to Yale.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.540
In fact, I remember the heated debates

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.010
over whether women should be included.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:54.040
The female to male ratio in
my class was about one to 10

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:57.593
but in science, i.e. my major,
was closer to one to 50.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.670
For the very few women
of color in my class,

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.600
I'm sure it was an even
more challenging situation.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.070
It's amazing to me now
that the Class of 2020

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.890
has slightly more women than men.

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.520
You likely heard a lot
about what it was like

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:14.260
given the celebration of women at Yale

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.190
that occurred last fall.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.940
At Mixers, busloads of women would arrive

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.470
from the Seven Sisters,
all female colleges

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.410
like Smith and Mount Holyoke,

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:25.513
to be ogled by the men in my class.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.230
Women athletes in my class lacked the same

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:32.600
kind of facilities and equipment
that the men were given.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:34.930
The experience, however,
introduced me to situations

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:37.490
that I faced over my
entire professional career

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.480
where there are far fewer women than men

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.560
who've made it to leadership positions,

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:43.910
particularly in the hard sciences.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.470
It has led me to proactive
and mentoring trainees,

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.980
whether they be male,
female or transgender.

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.350
And it gives me special
empathy for the pressures

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:54.310
and intimidation that
racial and ethnic minorities

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:56.853
and sometimes now white men face today.

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:59.290
Before deciding to go to Yale,

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:01.370
I worried, what would it be like

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:03.210
with my parents being there.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:06.050
There were only a few events
that drew embarrassment.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:09.310
For example, my brother Bill,
who was in the Class of ’78,

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:12.101
at Berkeley College, heard that
dad had been seen swimming

00:13:12.101 --> 00:13:16.100
with his fins pushing a
bathroom scale through the pool.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:18.190
We were quite embarrassed.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:20.840
Dad had been collecting
data on drag coefficients

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.100
in different environments
for his computer class

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.660
that he was teaching.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:25.697
Bill called him and said,

00:13:25.697 --> 00:13:29.337
"Dad, I hear you've been weighing
yourself in the Yale pool,

00:13:29.337 --> 00:13:31.207
"would you cut it out?"

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:35.190
Despite those minimal challenges,

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.090
I had a fantastic Yale College experience.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:40.340
I took full advantage
of new opportunities,

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:42.260
many unique to Yale,

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.670
I got a job washing
glassware in the laboratory

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:47.510
of a famous cell
biologist, Dr. Joseph Gall

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:49.540
and ultimately managed to parlay

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.950
that into a wonderful
independent research experience

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.620
that made me decide to
go to graduate school.

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:56.790
I also learned how to
follow new developments

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:58.230
as they're being made.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.810
My husband Albert, some
years later asked me

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.980
how I learned molecular biology
because only the rudiments

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:06.770
existed when I got my start at Yale.

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:09.320
Al if I took a Cold Spring Harbor course,

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.740
very few of the tools, for
example, restriction enzymes

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:15.090
or PCR, were available.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.233
I just learned them as they
were developed and described.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:20.800
Some of you have asked why I went

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.130
to medical school after graduate school.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.080
Honestly, I'm living proof
that you can alter your career

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:28.940
trajectory as circumstances change.

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.280
I realized that as recombinant DNA tools

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.160
are being developed, that it
might eventually be possible

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:36.650
to treat disease at its root.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.820
I had met somebody who
was planning to do this,

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:41.580
and I decided that's what I wanna do.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.640
How else do you learn
about the diseases amenable

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.740
to treatment than to go to medical school?

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.200
On our first day of medical school,

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:50.950
the dean spoke to us
about a terrible disease,

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.700
a disease that didn't even have a name

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:56.330
that was affecting
Haitians and homosexuals.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:58.390
During our clerkships, my fellow students

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:00.450
and I were in the trenches, night and day.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:03.730
Taking care of dying young
men, drawing blood gases

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:06.140
doing cultures of every bodily fluid

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:08.560
with barely any protective gear.

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:10.930
At our graduation ceremony, my classmates

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:14.140
and newlywed husband,
Albert McGuire was selected

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.320
to give the class commencement speech

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.420
where he described our experience learning

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:21.110
about this devastating disease
which had recently been

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:24.360
given a name, HIV/AIDS.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.700
I can't help but think
back to those days now

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:28.810
with the current pandemic in mind.

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.240
Of course, current evidence
shows that SARS-CoV-2

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:36.360
is far more infectious and more
immediately deadly than HIV.

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:39.120
But like SARS-CoV-2, we didn't know much

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.000
about the biology of HIV.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:42.970
We didn't have a treatment.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:46.000
It will take time but
hopefully not too much time

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:49.450
to learn what to do to treat
and prevent COVID-19 disease

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:50.523
and we're on our way.

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:53.820
In any case, what Albert and I learned

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.090
is that medical training
prepares people to work

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:59.340
at a high level with
incredible stress and endurance

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.360
and to put the patient first.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.810
We've seen this recently
starting in Wuhan,

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:07.370
then Lombardy and recently New
York City and other cities.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:09.200
Many of our medical school classmates

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:12.670
including Dr. Nancy Brown,
the distinguished new dean

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:16.040
of Yale Medical School, and
also a Yale College alumna

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.000
are leading these battles.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.350
Another one of the first
Yale College Coeds,

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:23.890
Dr. Nancy Berliner is leading
the charge at the Dana Farber

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:27.210
and Brigham and Women's Hospital in Boston.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:29.790
These leaders and others and
the thousands of doctors,

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:32.190
nurses and support staff
working day and night

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:33.840
are our heroes.

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:36.330
If you talk to them,
although many are terrified

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.223
and exhausted, they say
they're just doing their job.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.890
Students asked me how does
one become a gene therapist,

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:46.890
gene therapy was still a fantasy

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.530
when I finished medical school,

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:51.100
so I had to develop my own training plan.

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:54.980
I was fortunate to experience
the Yale campus again

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.040
during my first year of that training plan

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.090
and the lab of the Yale
Medical School dean at the time

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.500
Dr. Leon Rosenberg, while
my husband Al completed

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.070
a surgery internship, we
had the unique opportunity

00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:08.910
to become Sillimanders for that year

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:13.310
while my father was master,
now known as Head of College.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:15.640
This allowed me to meet
many amazing students,

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.143
many of whom have stayed
in touch with my family.

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:21.200
And further training at Johns Hopkins,

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:24.570
we narrowed in on our
target tissue, the retina.

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:27.430
It was at UPenn, where we
were able to turn the fantasy

00:17:27.430 --> 00:17:30.960
of using genes to treat
disease into a reality.

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:33.170
Of course, to carry out gene therapy,

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.890
you need to know the genes
and how to deliver them.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.930
At that point, we lacked both the genes

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:40.390
and the tools to deliver
them efficiently and safely

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:42.100
to organs or animals.

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.360
Did that stop us?

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:44.600
No way.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:48.003
That's where naiveté
and energy are an asset.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.570
What was it like in those early days?

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:54.070
We did our first studies in biohazard gear

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:56.610
such as what you commonly
see now in the news,

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.930
in cubicles with animals with potentially

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:01.120
deadly infectious diseases.

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:03.190
This is because no one
knew whether the reagents

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:06.700
we were testing were
safe, we de-risk them.

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:09.230
We figured out how to
accomplish the surgical delivery

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:11.529
of gene therapy reagents to the retina,

00:18:11.529 --> 00:18:15.620
evaluate immune response and
stability of gene transfer,

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:18.590
what models would be best
and how to evaluate function

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:21.130
and safety of our newly delivered genes.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:23.430
The science was the easy part.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.340
Other issues were more challenging,

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:28.303
such as trying to get a
foothold on the faculty ladder.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:32.500
The odds were and continue
to be particularly low

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:36.090
in the sciences for a woman
at times with the demands

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.960
and also didn't desire to
be with my three children

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.130
who were then under six years of age,

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.720
it seemed impossible to compete.

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:46.380
I scoured my institution
for female role models

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:49.160
and I found only two
female full professors.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.700
Numerous other female
scientists had been discouraged

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:53.830
from proceeding and
ended up running the labs

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:55.810
of their male colleagues.

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:58.540
One of the two female
professors who had made it

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:00.670
told me that her idea of a summer vacation

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:04.470
was to leave the lab at 5p.m
during the last week of August,

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:07.140
most other day and she left at 11p.m.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.500
Both of them told me that
they decided long ago

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:10.950
never to have children.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:14.930
Well, I had kids, so I had
to just try to defy the odds.

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:16.313
What did I have to lose?

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:20.070
We found in our studies
that we can make puppies

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:23.441
that had been born blind,
see for the very first time.

00:19:23.441 --> 00:19:26.290
This was through a one time
delivery of the correct copy

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:29.110
of their defective gene
to their blind retinas.

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:33.300
It was a eureka moment to see
a puppy curled up in a corner

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:34.950
scared of bumping into something

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:38.050
because it couldn't see and
then to see it running around

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.450
catching toys and playing
with its sighted friends

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.590
after a single treatment.

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:45.180
We realized the importance
of this finding immediately

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:48.440
as did laymen. One of
the gene therapy dogs,

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:51.170
Lancelot, appeared on Good Morning America.

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:53.610
He attended dinner dances
at the New York Hilton

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:55.860
and presented his story
three different times

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:59.920
to Congress about the importance
of biomedical research.

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:02.470
He and other canine
participants ended up living

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.900
to old age still seeing
two of them in our home.

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:07.700
We adopted them at the end of the study.

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:10.460
Of course our thought was,
wouldn't it be incredible

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:15.280
to make blind children see, we
immediately started planning,

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.120
but had not anticipated a
disaster in the gene therapy field

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:22.580
would bring everything
to a screeching halt.

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:26.060
The disaster was the death of
the first gene therapy patient

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:28.750
with another disease and
not one of our patients.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:31.630
But it was at my own institution.

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:35.190
This was damning for the
field of gene therapy.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:37.650
The FDA halted all gene therapy trials,

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:40.740
and there were public
meetings and scathing letters.

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:43.620
All of the funding dried
up, gene therapy companies

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:47.073
went broke and investigators
abandoned their projects.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:50.890
Because of the inhospitable
climate for gene therapy,

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.500
there were now numerous
hurdles in our way.

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.660
The safety constraints
had been ratcheted up

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.850
so that there were 13 different committees

00:20:57.850 --> 00:20:59.950
to navigate conflict of interest,

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:01.660
pharmacy devise, et cetera.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.710
And then even a committee
overseeing those committees

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:08.620
before we could even complete
the institutional paperwork.

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:09.980
We had no funding.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:11.750
But we were fortunate to have an advocate

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:14.360
and Dr. Katherine High of
The Children's Hospital

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:16.510
of Philadelphia, CHOP.

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:20.290
CHOP realize that the majority
of pediatric onset diseases

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:23.563
are genetic in origin and
decided to invest in it.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.880
We perfected our reagent
showed that it reproducibly

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:30.790
and reliably restored
vision in our animal models

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:34.730
and was safe, and we addressed
one challenge after another.

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:37.800
These included legal issues
about inclusion of children

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.960
in clinical trials, developing
new outcome measures,

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:44.440
establishing statistical
analysis protocols

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:47.250
for demonstrating efficacy
in this rare disease,

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:49.360
and addressing all the unknowns relative

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:51.320
to rational gene therapy.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:55.110
Some of these seem strange
in retrospect, for example,

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:58.610
what items should be included
in the informed consent.

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:01.410
Would blind individuals
find it distressing

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:04.050
to be able to see after treatment?

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:07.990
If so we could offer to remove
their newly gained vision,

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:11.510
something that seemed
unthinkable to the subjects.

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:13.750
Would the gene therapy
reagent have the potential

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:16.190
of being passed on to offspring?

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:18.490
We and the parents chuckled as we made sure

00:22:18.490 --> 00:22:20.610
that the eight year old
boys in our study knew

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:22.760
that they would not be
allowed to father children

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:23.863
for the next year.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:29.060
There's only been one gauge
of success, the eye chart

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:31.160
that doesn't work for the blind.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.440
Instead, I assembled an
obstacle course with steps,

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:38.080
turns and doors before
surgery, the patient failed

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:43.080
after it they navigated
perfectly, that convinced the FDA.

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:47.120
We initiated our human
clinical trial in October 2007,

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:49.900
the father of one of your
Yale 2020 classmates,

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:51.640
Emma Pierce, assisted.

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:53.880
The volunteers described
numerous new things

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.130
that they could see.

00:22:55.130 --> 00:22:57.070
However, we remained cautious until

00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:00.110
we had the first objective data in hand.

00:23:00.110 --> 00:23:03.470
When I first saw the definitive
data after graphing it out

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:07.610
late one night, I ran and woke
Al up to tell him. It was 3a.m.

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:10.290
But he didn't mind being woken for this.

00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:13.590
In the glow of this
success, my father asked me

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:16.690
whether this work might
benefit the two blind students

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:18.580
he had known in Silliman.

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:21.447
I told him, "Dad, the
chances are really slim

00:23:21.447 --> 00:23:23.677
"because this gene defect is rare.

00:23:23.677 --> 00:23:25.860
"It probably wouldn't help them."

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:28.440
A few months later, my father died.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:31.320
And then shortly after his
death, I received a message

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:34.570
that the blindness of those
two individuals was due

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:37.993
to the very gene defect
that we were studying.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:42.740
Although when we started,
we just wanted to see

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.160
if this would work, we
realized that once it did work,

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:47.820
we had to follow it to the
very end, drug approval

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:49.890
so that others could benefit.

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:52.790
Of course, we had not received
any training in this process,

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:54.840
nor was there a path.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:58.830
However, we persevered with
every obstacle thrown our way.

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:01.760
It was an emotional moment
when we received unanimous

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:06.750
approval by the FDA for the
drug Luxturna in December 2017,

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:09.330
and then worldwide the following year.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:11.930
We were truly the fortunate
ones to be able to witness

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:14.050
the dramatic life changes experienced

00:24:14.050 --> 00:24:16.320
by these courageous pioneers.

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:19.540
Being able to put aside their
Braille books and blind canes,

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:21.420
go to school, get a job,

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:24.260
see their children's
faces for the first time,

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:28.000
live independently,
ski, hike, you name it.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.990
We were so lucky to be in the
situation to know these people

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:34.490
and to witness such improvements
in their vision in lives.

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:37.090
My husband routinely treats
people with this inherited

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:40.210
blindness now with this
first approved gene therapy,

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:43.230
we never tired of hearing their
stories of what they can do

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.980
now that they can see well,
and we've been working

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:48.653
to develop the next
generation of gene therapies.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.360
With the current coronavirus situation,

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.220
I, like scientists here at Yale
and all over the world I've

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:58.310
had to shut down my lab
as only research relevant

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:01.190
to COVID-19 has been declared essential.

00:25:01.190 --> 00:25:03.350
This is where some of my Yale experience

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:05.603
and grit and determination kicked in.

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:09.570
After a few days of anxiety,
shock and depression,

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.780
I began reading what is known
about the molecular biology,

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:17.040
virology, immunology and
pathology of SARS-CoV-2,

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:19.560
I started talking to one
of the lead collaborators

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:21.930
who himself is a Yale College graduate,

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:23.880
and who I learned recently has a daughter

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:27.760
Sara in this class,
congratulation Tabins.

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:30.600
We discussed how to apply
and pivot our expertise

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:33.520
and resources start working on this.

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:36.500
It didn't take long for us to
establish the preliminary set

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:40.350
of studies aiming to develop
a safe and effective vaccine.

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:43.020
We have no guarantee if
we will be successful,

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:46.080
but if we don't try we
won't have anything.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:47.700
This effort has energized me

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:49.960
and I'm sure this resonates with you.

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:54.420
We do not want to have a future
overshadowed by coronavirus.

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:56.960
I know that at some point
we will be able to get back

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:59.030
to the projects we put on hold.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:01.610
In the meantime, each of
us can try to figure out

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:03.410
how to apply our skills the best

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:05.773
or to do what we can to contribute.

00:26:07.727 --> 00:26:10.210
I truly believe that my
Yale education prepared me

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:12.910
to reconfigure when appropriate
and take on challenges

00:26:12.910 --> 00:26:16.300
as they appear. To have the
bold hopes, perseverance,

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.970
determination, thick skin and patience

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:21.210
to see projects through.

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.130
Some members of your class have asked me

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:25.920
whether they'll ever see
their friends from Yale again.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:28.720
Graduates, not a problem.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:31.090
I've stayed in touch with
faculty members and friends

00:26:31.090 --> 00:26:33.030
from numerous Yale experiences.

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:35.530
From the dining hall, roommates, classes,

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:38.100
the Yale Symphony, from
attending performances

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:40.390
and museums and athletic events.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:43.160
I've rekindled connections at reunions,

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:46.090
my undergraduate friends and
I have shared life events,

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:49.940
weddings, births of children,
promotions, shows, concerts,

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:53.070
career changes, sad events.

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:55.030
They're like extended family.

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:57.540
In fact, some of the
first friends I contacted

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:01.400
when the pandemic hit were my
Yale undergraduate friends.

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:05.333
Those relationships are
enduring and ever expanding.

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:10.540
We are all now faced with an
opportunity for reflection.

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:14.150
The world has been so divided
but for once our stress

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:18.140
is the same stress, something
that binds us together.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.300
Our views of safety have changed.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:22.260
Instead of being scared of others,

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:25.400
we worry about being the
unwitting mules of harm.

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:28.620
Being Mary Mallon, aka Typhoid Mary,

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:31.010
the cook who silently spread typhoid fever

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:34.510
to more than 50 people in the early 1900s.

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:37.310
Conversely, those of us
who have been healthy

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:39.630
don't want to experience the disease.

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:42.890
We or at least most of us
have been following guidelines

00:27:42.890 --> 00:27:45.493
for social distancing
and protective steps. [dog barks]

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:53.530
Who would have thought that
seeing everyone wearing masks

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:55.330
would be the new norm?

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:58.320
Now when I see images of
groups of people without masks,

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:02.100
I wonder where are those
people that they are not concerned?

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:05.823
It is heartening to see the
solidarity concern and respect.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:09.610
Some of the changes I've
noticed in recent weeks

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:11.430
are truly beautiful.

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:14.120
It seems to me that people
have become more outgoing

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:15.490
and more caring.

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:18.300
They're bringing groceries
and medicines to those in need

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:20.440
lending a helping hand, even if there are

00:28:20.440 --> 00:28:24.650
some kooky elements like stop
stockpiling toilet paper.

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:26.500
I've had the opportunity to meet neighbors

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:29.640
we never even knew existed
while walking my dogs.

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:31.360
People call out from across the street

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:33.280
to ask how the family's doing.

00:28:33.280 --> 00:28:35.960
People are more appreciative and thankful.

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:39.300
We see images and videos of
groups cheering and clapping

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:41.510
for people who've saved lives.

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:44.380
Instead of going out to
restaurants, people are cooking

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:46.650
and trying new recipes despite worries

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:49.560
about gaining the COVID-20.

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:51.080
Children have time to be kids

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.840
instead of rushing from event to event.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:55.800
They're riding their bikes
around the neighborhood

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:57.870
without helicopter parents.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:00.280
Part of the reason is likely
that there's no traffic

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:02.090
and it's much safer for them.

00:29:02.090 --> 00:29:04.740
Plus, maybe their parents
don't mind having a few minutes

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:05.903
of peace and quiet.

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:09.580
It is nice to be able to take
advantage of new technologies

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:11.830
to meet instead of flying everywhere.

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:14.210
The sky is even bluer for it and our world

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:16.820
has less machine made noise.

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:20.290
I hear reports of dolphins
swimming in the canals of Venice,

00:29:20.290 --> 00:29:23.270
and I'm skeptical of them
until I step outside my home

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:25.970
and I see a fox running
across my neighbor's yard

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:28.430
and a deer with her
fawn walking slowly down

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:29.763
the middle of the street.

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:33.620
The IT world has proven
over and over again

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:37.040
and right now in fact
shout out to Rick Leone

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:40.508
and Douglas Forbush that
they can keep us all connected,

00:29:40.508 --> 00:29:43.990
whether it be in classes,
performances, art exhibits,

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:46.820
lectures, births or Class Day.

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:49.940
It's strange and confusing
and funny and surreal

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:52.010
and endearing and heartening,

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:54.770
giving us all a feeling of connection.

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:56.870
We now have the demonstrated opportunities

00:29:56.870 --> 00:29:59.909
for carrying out Virtual
Education, telemedicine,

00:29:59.909 --> 00:30:02.680
thesis defenses and
for sharing artwork,

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:05.450
we can take virtual tours of Notre Dame,

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:09.160
we can attend livestream
performances of symphonies.

00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:11.770
Attendance has never been higher.

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:14.970
We see Lady Gaga, Mick
Jagger, Taylor Swift

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:17.240
and other stars performing
in their basements,

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:19.330
and we're entertained
by comedians appearing

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:22.200
in their kitchens on Saturday Night Live.

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:24.950
There's baseball in
ballparks without fans

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:28.200
online support groups, dance
classes, weightlifting,

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:31.820
body combat, improvisation, memes,

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:35.690
and I even saw poker games
on the Yale Facebook page.

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:37.580
The coronavirus cruelly robbed you

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:40.360
of those very last days at Yale.

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:43.060
This is a huge deal and certainly nothing

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:45.270
that any of us anticipated.

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:47.330
However, do not despair.

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:50.070
This terrible experience has
created new opportunities

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:52.900
and your class will shape things to come.

00:30:52.900 --> 00:30:55.890
This event will galvanize
you all to put your talents,

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:59.190
expertise, imagination
and passions to good use.

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.740
You have a huge opportunity for a reset

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:03.720
to re-engineer our world.

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:07.050
Disaster makes the
impossible become possible.

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:09.900
The pandemic has shown us
how quickly things can change

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:11.300
if they must.

00:31:11.300 --> 00:31:14.430
We are still in shock,
adjusting, grieving, mourning

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:17.900
and recalibrating, but Class of 2020,

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:20.500
soon we will need you all to step in

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:22.390
and roll up your sleeves.

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:25.470
The way one deals with obstacles
is often what sets apart

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:28.330
those that accomplish their
goals and those who don't.

00:31:28.330 --> 00:31:31.020
Of course, a little luck
helps but if we don't try,

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:32.730
we don't succeed.

00:31:32.730 --> 00:31:34.070
Although it can be frustrating,

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:37.060
we all learn from the challenges
that are placed in our path

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:39.320
and the mistakes we make along the way.

00:31:39.320 --> 00:31:42.290
We learn from being tested
and going through disruptions.

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:43.840
We learn how to be independent

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:46.800
as well as how to collaborate
and work with others.

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:48.510
It's okay to worry a little bit.

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:51.730
That's a protective mechanism
as long as it's in check,

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:54.890
but be assured that this
long and winding road

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:57.830
will lead us to
opportunities and solutions.

00:31:57.830 --> 00:32:00.100
It may take time for
each of you to recognize

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:03.340
where you best fit in, but
there are numerous opportunities

00:32:03.340 --> 00:32:05.630
AC, after corona.

00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:07.650
For the short term, you can take advantage

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:10.460
of having the time and
physical isolation to read

00:32:10.460 --> 00:32:13.720
and think and to fill in the
gaps in your knowledge base.

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:16.130
It's also important to
spend the time on yourself

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:19.160
to develop your talents,
whether they be to write, draw,

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:22.673
sing, dance, we won't have
this opportunity forever.

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:26.500
Many of you are nurturing,
encouraging and inspiring

00:32:26.500 --> 00:32:29.560
your friends, siblings,
parents and grandparents,

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:31.500
what a great opportunity to strengthen

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:34.060
those unique relationships.

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:37.070
Those who have suffered
terrible COVID-19 symptoms

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:40.020
are going to need extra physical
and psychological support

00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:42.873
from all of us to get well
and independent again.

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:47.103
People are going to say
we will get through this.

00:32:47.103 --> 00:32:49.370
That is not enough.

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:52.010
We need to think about the long term.

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:54.610
For those of you who carry
out scientific research

00:32:54.610 --> 00:32:55.990
do not fear.

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:58.540
The majority of you who
have worked in research labs

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:02.200
have had to curtail those
relationships due to coronavirus.

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:05.730
We need you, get ready,
things will crank up sooner

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:07.330
than you think.

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:08.530
For those in other fields,

00:33:08.530 --> 00:33:10.650
there will be numerous opportunities.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:13.730
We depend on you to unite
our fragmented government,

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:16.210
to use your economic and financial acumen

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:18.380
to address financial problems,

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:21.080
to foster business
opportunities that are honest

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:22.730
and not self serving.

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:25.950
To treat our workers properly,
keep them out of harm's way

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:30.290
and give them a livable minimum
wage, daycare and sick leave.

00:33:30.290 --> 00:33:32.920
To take care of our precious
Earth and provide habitats

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:37.070
for creatures and birds, to
take care of the homeless,

00:33:37.070 --> 00:33:39.690
impoverished, the mentally ill
and those who are addicted

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:42.540
to narcotics. To provide everyone

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:44.760
with a high quality education.

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.080
We need to stand up to
bullies and to make sure

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:49.270
that there's no interference
from foreign governments

00:33:49.270 --> 00:33:50.780
in our elections.

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:52.790
Remember to vote.

00:33:52.790 --> 00:33:55.700
And we need to support all of
those people who bring beauty,

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:58.000
joy and meaning to our lives every day

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:02.290
through music, art, literature,
comedy and performance.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:05.770
Move over scattered, boastful,
incoherent and unscientific

00:34:05.770 --> 00:34:09.380
behavior and make way for
compassion and what Camus

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:13.890
describes in The Plague as being
the most important, decency.

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:16.110
Above all, there is hope.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:17.050
We have all learned

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:19.320
and reflected on what is really important,

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.730
our relationships with one another.

00:34:21.730 --> 00:34:24.920
We can all draw inspiration
from those who have surmounted

00:34:24.920 --> 00:34:27.930
enormous obstacles to
arrive at our new normal,

00:34:27.930 --> 00:34:30.560
and make that even better, more just

00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:33.380
and more secure than the old normal.

00:34:33.380 --> 00:34:36.350
You're such an extraordinarily
resilient and diverse

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:39.390
and brave class, Class of 2020.

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:44.330
You're wired, pragmatic,
driven, empathetic and creative.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:46.160
You have shown through the recent events,

00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:49.320
how we are connected and that
there come extraordinary times

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:51.860
when we're impelled to
take care of one another

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:54.750
in a basic and profound way.

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:57.780
The pandemic has already taught
you to think beyond your own

00:34:57.780 --> 00:35:00.990
past expectations, to reformulate them

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:02.610
and begin the process of becoming

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:05.020
the next generation of leaders.

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.300
I applaud each and every one of you,

00:35:08.300 --> 00:35:11.740
my heartiest congratulations
and my very best wishes

00:35:11.740 --> 00:35:16.053
for your continued good health,
happiness and connectedness.

00:35:18.760 --> 00:35:21.330
- Thank you, Dr. Bennett
for your inspiring words.

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:23.100
Your message is especially powerful

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:24.970
in the current situation.

00:35:24.970 --> 00:35:27.250
In fact, it's at times like
these when we appreciate

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:29.970
even more those figures who
have been so instrumental

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:32.610
in our daily lives on campus,
and have really helped

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:35.820
to make Yale feel like home
over the past couple of years.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:37.880
Being away from campus now just makes us

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:41.660
remember how much we value
their love and support.

00:35:41.660 --> 00:35:44.370
At the same time, we also
are immensely grateful

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:47.620
to those who have been so key to making

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:50.310
this virtual graduation ceremony possible.

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:53.550
They have put in countless hours
to try to make our ceremony

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:57.680
as special as possibly can
be given the circumstances

00:35:57.680 --> 00:36:00.700
and we are so, so grateful.

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:04.510
And finally, to all of our
families and loved ones

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:06.070
who have helped us and supported us

00:36:06.070 --> 00:36:08.280
all the way to this important milestone.

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:09.500
Thank you so much.

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:11.370
We truly could not be here without you.

00:36:11.370 --> 00:36:13.690
And this is a celebration of you as well.

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:15.023
Thank you so much.

00:36:16.850 --> 00:36:20.010
- And to everyone, family and
friends watching from home.

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:22.080
Thank you for joining us this week.

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:23.880
We're excited to continue celebrating

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:25.820
when we come together in the future.

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:28.480
Until then, we hope that
you and your loved ones

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:30.830
stay safe and healthy.

00:36:30.830 --> 00:36:33.090
To conclude our Class Day celebrations,

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:36.110
we ask that you'll join us in
singing the Yale University

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:39.030
alma mater, "Bright College Years”.

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:41.020
We encourage you to take
out the handkerchief

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:43.210
you received in the Class Day box,

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:46.720
or the one you might still have
from our first week at Yale.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:49.320
We now present to you
"Bright College Years",

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:52.200
led by the senior members
of the Yale Glee Club,

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:54.110
as directed by Jeffrey Douma,

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:56.490
and the members of the
Yale Bands as directed

00:36:56.490 --> 00:36:58.150
by Thomas Duffy.

00:36:58.150 --> 00:37:00.700
Thank you all and we're
glad to have celebrated

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:03.927
these bright college years with
each and every one of you.

00:37:08.908 --> 00:37:11.491
(upbeat music)

00:37:29.914 --> 00:37:34.914
♪ Bright college years
with pleasure rife ♪

00:37:35.544 --> 00:37:40.544
♪ The shortest, gladdest years of life ♪

00:37:40.551 --> 00:37:45.366
♪ How swiftly are ye gliding by ♪

00:37:45.366 --> 00:37:50.366
♪ O why does time so quickly fly ♪

00:37:50.996 --> 00:37:55.996
♪ The seasons come, the seasons go ♪

00:37:56.618 --> 00:38:01.618
♪ The earth is green or white with snow ♪

00:38:02.705 --> 00:38:07.205
♪ But time and change shall not avail ♪

00:38:13.993 --> 00:38:18.826
♪ To break the friendships
formed at Yale ♪

00:38:24.038 --> 00:38:29.038
♪ In after years should trouble rise ♪

00:38:29.524 --> 00:38:34.524
♪ To cloud the blue of sunny skies ♪

00:38:34.698 --> 00:38:39.698
♪ How bright will seem
through mem'ry's haze ♪

00:38:39.796 --> 00:38:44.796
♪ Those happy golden bygone days ♪

00:38:45.208 --> 00:38:50.208
♪ O let us strive that ever we ♪

00:38:50.644 --> 00:38:55.644
♪ May let these words our watch cry be ♪

00:38:57.635 --> 00:39:02.052
♪ Where e'er upon life's sea we sail ♪

00:39:08.925 --> 00:39:13.342
♪ For God, for country, and for Yale ♪

00:39:22.846 --> 00:39:25.763
(crowd applauding)

00:39:41.590 --> 00:39:44.173
(upbeat music)

